Thursday Update
*&nbsp; Exited GM calls*&nbsp;Stocks slide as Fed comments fail to quell in=
flation fearsStocks are falling and government bond yields jumping even as =
Federal Reserve Chairman Jerome Powell said a buildup of some inflationary =
pressures in the economy was unlikely to result in a tighter monetary polic=
y.The Dow Jones Industrial Average fell by 218 points, or 0.7%, to 31,056. =
S&amp;P 500 was down by 1% to 3,780 and Nasdaq was down by 1.8% to 12,764 a=
fter all three indexes traded up earlier in the session before the Powell s=
peech. Consumer discretionary and technology were among the steepest declin=
ers while energy was the biggest gainer midday on Thursday.The US 10-year y=
ield jumped by 6.4 basis points to 1.53% after the Fed speech, heading towa=
rd its intraday high of 1.61% last week when concern mounted higher inflati=
on will force the Fed's hands into tightening monetary policy faster than t=
he market anticipates. President Joe Biden's $1.9 trillion program is curre=
ntly awaiting the Senate's approval.The "startling" jump on Wednesday in th=
e ISM services price index, which has been a decent guide to core inflation=
 since the crash in 2008, is the "main story" as the February reading signa=
ls inflation will rise above 2.5% over the next few months, Ian Shepherdson=
, chief economist at Pantheon Macroeconomics, said in a report Thursday."Th=
is is the first credible direct indicator we know which suggests that infla=
tion will rise above the rate implied by the huge base effects which will k=
ick-in on the anniversary of the pandemic," Shepherdson said.Powell told a =
WSJ conference that he sees a build-up some inflationary pressures ahead bu=
t does not expect it to be strong enough to spur the central bank to increa=
se interest rates."We expect that as the economy reopens and hopefully pick=
s up, we will see inflation move up through base effects," Powell said. =E2=
=80=9CThat could create some upward pressure on prices."West Texas Intermed=
iate futures surged by 4.6% to $64.24 a barrel after the Organization of th=
e Petroleum Exporting Countries reportedly decided to roll over existing pr=
oduction cuts throughout April, implying producers are not ready yet to tes=
t the market with increased supplies even as oil prices have risen above pr=
e-pandemic levels.On the S&amp;P 500 index, all the top-10 performers intra=
day were energy sector or related firms such as EOG Resources (EOG), and Ma=
rathon Oil (MRO), with each of three stocks up by at least 7.5%.Portfolio: =
&nbsp;Our order for DY calls did not fill on Wednesday. GM calls filled but=
 then stalled and flipped over to record a -29% loss on stop. Bummer to los=
e positions on stop two days in a row, but it=E2=80=99s not uncommon. The p=
oint is to keep losses at a minimum while waiting for positive streaks of 2=
5%-plus winners to materialize. No new recommendations amid the chop.&nbsp;=
*CURRENT RECOMMENDATIONSGeneral Motors (GM) March $50 calls-- Symbol: GM210=
319C00050000-- Bought at $4.10 on Wednesday-- Stopped at $2.90 today for a =
29% loss*Thanks for reading,Jon D. MarkmanAll recommendations are for month=
ly options expiring on the third Friday of the specified month unless other=
wise noted. All stops are good after 10:30 a.m. Eastern only unless otherwi=
se specified. All orders are good for the day only unless otherwise noted. =
"Day only" orders expire at the close of the current session. Past results =
are no guarantee of future returns. All accounts may not achieve comparable=
 results. Recommendations are leveraged and speculative, so only use risk c=
apital =E2=80=94 i.e., money you can afford to lose. All results are shown =
ex-commissions and fees. Options trading involves the risk of substantial l=
oss. Some news and research provided by Wall Street Journal, Bloomberg, Ass=
ociated Press, MT Newswires and Reuters.&nbsp;

